--- a character outside the alphabet
'QU I=' -> invalid base64 character ' ' at position 2
'QUI\n' -> invalid base64 character '\n' at position 3
'QUJD\r\n' -> invalid base64 character '\r' at position 4
'ab\x00d' -> invalid base64 character '\x00' at position 2
'é===' -> invalid base64 character 'é' at position 0
b'ab\xffd' -> invalid base64 character b'\xff' at position 2
'QU*=' -> invalid base64 character '*' at position 2
--- the other alphabet is refused, and named
'QUJ-' -> invalid base64 character '-' at position 3: that is the URL-safe alphabet, which is url=true
'QUJ_' -> invalid base64 character '_' at position 3: that is the URL-safe alphabet, which is url=true
'ab+/' -> invalid base64 character '+' at position 2: that is the standard alphabet, and url=true is the URL-safe one
'ab/c' -> invalid base64 character '/' at position 2: that is the standard alphabet, and url=true is the URL-safe one
--- the length
'A' -> base64 text length 1 is not a multiple of 4
'QQ' -> base64 text length 2 is not a multiple of 4
'QQ=' -> base64 text length 3 is not a multiple of 4
'QUJDQ' -> base64 text length 5 is not a multiple of 4
--- the padding
'Q===' -> base64 text ends in 3 padding characters at position 1; at most 2 are allowed
'====' -> base64 text ends in 4 padding characters at position 0; at most 2 are allowed
'QQ=A' -> base64 padding at position 2 is followed by data at position 3
'=QQQ' -> base64 padding at position 0 is followed by data at position 1
'QUI=QUJD' -> base64 padding at position 3 is followed by data at position 4
'QUJDQQ==QUJD' -> base64 padding at position 6 is followed by data at position 8
--- reading order: the first thing wrong is the one reported
'Q=-' -> invalid base64 character '-' at position 2: that is the URL-safe alphabet, which is url=true
'Q==' -> base64 text length 3 is not a multiple of 4
--- argument shapes
base64.encode() data must be bytes or str, not <class 'int'>
base64.decode() text must be bytes or str, not <class 'list'>
base64.encode() url= is true or false, not <class 'int'>
encode(): url= must be bool, not null — null does not mean "omitted"; leave url= out for the default
decode() takes 1 positional argument but 2 were given — write `decode('QQ==', url=true)`: a parameter with a default is passed by name
encode() got 'data' by name, but 'data' has no default and is passed by position — write `encode(b'x')`
<class 'str'> <class 'bytes'> <class 'bytes'>
